1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,809
Same robot.

2
00:00:00,809 --> 00:00:01,545
Same task.

3
00:00:01,545 --> 00:00:03,604
One run succeeds, one fails.

4
00:00:03,604 --> 00:00:06,988
Move the can three centimeters, and it misses.

5
00:00:06,988 --> 00:00:11,475
So which build do you ship, and what still blocks deployment?

6
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:16,368
 Quant Gate answers three questions.

7
00:00:16,368 --> 00:00:19,236
Which candidate do I validate first?

8
00:00:19,236 --> 00:00:20,988
Can this build deploy?

9
00:00:20,988 --> 00:00:22,900
And what do I test next?

10
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:27,934
It started with an arm that brings a drink to someone working hard.

11
00:00:27,934 --> 00:00:34,505
This S O one oh one runs an A C T policy on a Jetson Orin Nano Super, trained on Nebius Jobs.

12
00:00:34,505 --> 00:00:43,690
From that one policy we built twenty-six TensorRT engines: full precision, half precision, and twenty-four int eight calibrations.

13
00:00:43,690 --> 00:00:49,625
Seven hundred and forty-five real-arm trials ran from sealed, interleaved schedules.

14
00:00:49,925 --> 00:00:52,639
First decision: what to validate first.

15
00:00:52,639 --> 00:01:00,225
Measured on this Jetson, half precision was the fastest engine and the cheapest per inference, not int eight.

16
00:01:02,425 --> 00:01:04,166
But cost is not approval.

17
00:01:04,166 --> 00:01:14,961
In the nominal tests, every candidate succeeded ninety to a hundred percent of the time, and the offline audit's top picks did no better than random picks.

18
00:01:14,961 --> 00:01:20,950
Twenty-five trials cannot tell them apart, so the verdict is: not enough evidence yet.

19
00:01:21,250 --> 00:01:24,760
We detected no success difference between the precisions.

20
00:01:24,760 --> 00:01:27,900
The clearest failures came from the physical setup.

21
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:30,795
Without the gripper pads, eight of twenty-four.

22
00:01:30,795 --> 00:01:33,258
A start moved three centimeters: missed.

23
00:01:33,258 --> 00:01:38,923
Fifty new demonstrations and a sealed comparison: twelve of twelve, against three of twelve.

24
00:01:38,923 --> 00:01:47,975
We repeated it for the next gaps, a smaller can and six centimeters out: twelve of twelve in both, against the first fine-tune in the same session.

25
00:01:48,275 --> 00:01:50,648
Even the camera mount is in scope.

26
00:01:50,648 --> 00:01:57,000
When success dropped, we kept every result sealed, and traced it to a knocked wrist camera.

27
00:01:57,575 --> 00:02:03,377
Cosmos Reason, on a Nebius A I Endpoint, judges the final frame of each trial.

28
00:02:03,377 --> 00:02:11,484
On ninety-six new trials, it caught twenty-two of twenty-four failures, with two false alarms in seventy-two.

29
00:02:11,484 --> 00:02:18,625
Nemotron Super on Token Factory explains the review, while code writes every number and verdict.

30
00:02:19,175 --> 00:02:21,702
Here is what a developer does with it.

31
00:02:21,702 --> 00:02:23,897
Pick a candidate: half precision.

32
00:02:23,897 --> 00:02:27,688
The evidence: cost on the device, and results on the arm.

33
00:02:27,688 --> 00:02:28,885
The verdict: hold.

34
00:02:28,885 --> 00:02:33,008
It is not approved, because the comparison is still too small.

35
00:02:33,008 --> 00:02:36,998
The plan says what to run next, under a pre-registered rule.

36
00:02:36,998 --> 00:02:43,250
And a build whose files no longer match what was audited is blocked before it reaches the arm.

37
00:02:44,175 --> 00:02:46,958
Validate half precision first.

38
00:02:46,958 --> 00:02:48,442
Hold deployment.

39
00:02:48,442 --> 00:02:50,297
Run the listed test.

40
00:02:50,297 --> 00:02:51,318
Quant Gate.

41
00:02:51,318 --> 00:02:54,750
Earn the next test, not the approval.
